From 9ae11afc42f12a7314fdbafef6b27b449f1e0204 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 21:21:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 106/108] drm/vino: add the DRM/KMS scanout engine

Add the KMS objects, the atomic-commit path and the scanout engine that
turns a compositor's framebuffer into the dock's wire format.

The dock cannot scan out a GEM object, so a commit copies the changed
strips into driver-owned coherent storage and only then completes,
releasing the source framebuffer back to userspace. Encoding and USB
submission continue asynchronously from that immutable snapshot. Nothing
blocking runs in the atomic callbacks: they record desired state and wake
per-head workers.

Send only what changed. Each strip's content is hashed during the snapshot,
and a strip whose hash moves is charged a transmission for every buffer the
dock rotates through, plus one. That debt is what a delta selects on -- not
merely this frame's damage -- because one presentation reaches exactly one
dock buffer, and a strip that arrives in some of them leaves the panel
alternating between old and new content. No analysis of the wire can see
it: the bytes are correct. An idle desktop therefore sends nothing at all,
which is also what the vendor driver does.

Encoding is fanned across CPUs as independent per-strip work items, and an
encoded strip body is reused while its content hash is unchanged, so the
retransmissions the debt owes do not re-run the codec.

Apply the CRTC's CTM and GAMMA_LUT in software while the pixels are still
here. A dock has no colour hardware to program, so a compositor correcting
through those properties has nowhere else to put the correction. color.rs
is shared verbatim with drm/evdi, which has the same problem; the copies
are kept byte-identical and a tool in the companion repository fails if
they drift.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Assisted-by: Codex:gpt-5
Signed-off-by: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
